          "id": "1.6",
          "title": "What Is Machine Learning?",
          "type": "theory",
          "content": "Artificial Intelligence (AI) is the broad goal of building systems that perform tasks requiring intelligence. Machine Learning (ML) is a subset of AI where a system learns patterns from data rather than following hand-written rules. Deep Learning is a subset of ML using multi-layer neural networks, particularly effective on unstructured data (images, text, audio).\n\n- Supervised learning: learn a mapping from inputs (features) to known outputs (labels/targets), e.g., predicting house price from square footage.\n- Unsupervised learning: find structure in data with no labels, e.g., grouping customers into segments.\n- Reinforcement learning: an agent learns by taking actions in an environment and receiving rewards.\n\nVocabulary you must know cold: a sample (one row/observation), features (input columns), label/target (what you're predicting), a model (the learned function mapping features → target), training (fitting the model to data), validation (tuning decisions on held-out data), testing (final, untouched performance check), inference (using a trained model on new data).\n\n**Why it matters.** The word \"learning\" is the most misleading word in our field: a model does not understand anything — it records statistical regularities between numbers. Analogy: ML is like teaching someone to recognize birds by showing them 10,000 labeled photos; the learner never learns \"what a bird is\", only \"which pixel patterns tend to co-occur with the bird label\".\n\n**Step-by-step intuition.** Induction is the core idea: from specific examples (training data) the model infers a general rule (a function from features to labels). The reason we split data into train/validation/test is that we must verify the rule generalizes to examples the model never saw — otherwise you are measuring memorization, not learning. Supervised = the teacher's answer key exists (labels). Unsupervised = no answer key, the algorithm hunts for structure. Reinforcement = learning by trial, error, and reward signals over time.\n\n**Practitioner notes.** Internalize the vocabulary until it is reflex: sample, feature, label, model, training, validation, testing, inference. These eight words are the entire field in miniature — every advanced concept (cross-validation, overfitting, data leakage, deployment) is a variation on one of them.",
