          "title": "Real-World Example",
          "type": "theory",
          "content": "A streaming service predicting \"will this user cancel next month?\" starts exactly here: raw event logs and billing records (Pandas), turned into numeric arrays (NumPy), fed into a supervised classification model (Module 4) — churn (yes/no) is the label, watch-time/payment-history are the features.\n\n**Why it matters.** Churn prediction is the classic \"first real project\" because it is a pure, self-contained supervised problem: you have historical data, a clear binary label (canceled or not), and a business metric (revenue saved). Walking the example through the workflow end-to-end now gives you a template you can map onto any future dataset.\n\n**Step-by-step intuition.** Follow the boxes: (1) Raw data — event logs and billing records arrive as messy tables; (2) Pandas — you load them, join on customer_id, and compute derived features like \"months since last payment\" or \"support tickets in 30 days\"; (3) NumPy — every feature becomes a numeric array and missing values become NaN to handle; (4) the label is \"churned\" (1) vs \"active\" (0) measured a fixed window after the features; (5) a classifier from Module 4 learns P(churn | features); (6) validation answers \"is the model better than just predicting the majority class?\"; (7) deployment scores each customer monthly and the business targets the top-decile risk group.\n\n**Practitioner notes.** Notice the design decisions hidden in that walkthrough: the definition of \"churned\", the feature window, and the deployment threshold are all business decisions you must make explicitly and document — they are where a data scientist's real judgment lives.",
