          "id": "1.9",
          "title": "Common Mistakes",
          "type": "theory",
          "content": "- Confusing = (assignment) with == (comparison) in conditions.\n- Forgetting that Pandas filtering (df[df.age > 30]) returns a copy by default, causing SettingWithCopyWarning on later edits.\n- Assuming a model \"understands\" data the way a human does — it only ever sees numbers.\n\n**Why it matters.** The mistakes in this lesson are the \"serial killers\" of ML code — quiet, common, and data-corrupting. None of them raises an error; they just produce subtly wrong results that surface as confusing model behavior days later.\n\n**Step-by-step intuition.** Walk through each one: (1) = vs == — assignment mutates a variable, comparison tests it; an if condition that assigns instead of compares evaluates a truthy value and silently always runs. (2) Chained assignment like df[df.age > 30][\"income\"] = 0 triggers Pandas' SettingWithCopyWarning because the intermediate selection can be a copy — the write lands nowhere, your data stays unmodified, and you may not notice. (3) NaN poisoning: np.nan != np.nan is True, so missing values never match themselves; always use pd.isna() or .dropna(). (4) The model-sees-numbers myth: passing text into a model that expects numbers will either crash or coerce silently (strings become object dtype), so encoding is a mandatory step, not an option.\n\n**Practitioner notes.** Cultivate the habit of asserting data invariants: df[\"age\"].isna().sum() == 0, X.shape[0] == y.shape[0], and df.dtypes after every preprocessing step. Three one-line asserts in the morning prevent three all-day debugging sessions.",
          "codeSnippet": null
