          "id": "5.1",
          "title": "Introduction",
          "type": "theory",
          "content": "A model that looks good on training data can be worthless in production. This module teaches the professional discipline of evaluating models honestly.\n\n**Why it matters.** Everything in the first four modules produced models; this module is where you prove they actually work — and it is the most professionally important module in the course. Companies hire for evaluation skill as much as modeling skill: the difference between a model that performs and one that merely looks like it performs is exactly the discipline taught here.\n\n**Step-by-step intuition.** The core problem: any metric computed on the training data is meaningless (memorization), and any metric computed on data you tuned against is optimistic. So the entire module is a set of techniques for one goal: estimate how the model will perform on data it has never seen, with minimal optimism and maximal trustworthiness. Splitting, cross-validation, and nested validation are three escalating levels of the same defense.\n\n**Practitioner notes.** Adopt the habit now: every experiment in every later module runs inside a train/validation/test frame. If you skip it \"just for this quick test\", you are training yourself into the exact behavior the module warns against.",
          "codeSnippet": null
